    "research": "Size curve, unisex bodies: blend of two published size-distribution breakdowns, an all-product aggregate (L 32, M 29, XL 20, S 12, 2XL 5, XS 1 percent) and a sales-derived ratio (XS 1, S 10, M 23, L 31, XL 23, 2XL 9, 3XL 3 percent, renormalised without 3XL). Both keep M/L/XL near 75 percent of units, which those guides give as the rule of thumb. Women's vest: the same guides' S 1 : M 2 : L 3 : XL 2 : 2XL 1 fleece bell curve, shifted one step smaller for a women's cut. Sources: https://www.threelayer.com/guides/size-ratio-guide-clothing-brands/ , https://manateeshirt.com/blog/average-tshirt-size-distribution , https://www.clothes2order.com/blog/how-to-order-the-right-amount-of-each-t-shirt-size/ . Colour curve: published sources agree on the ORDERING for basics (black leads, navy and dark heather grey follow close behind and are roughly interchangeable) but publish no percentage split, so 40/30/30 is a ranked assumption, not a measured distribution. It is corroborated by this store's own on-hand mix at derivation time (crewneck black 39, navy 33, grey 28 percent; quarter-zip black 43, navy 32, grey 25 percent) and is the weakest input in this file; the demand pass exists to replace it with observed sales. Sources: https://www.printful.com/blog/most-popular-t-shirt-colors , https://printify.com/blog/most-popular-shirt-colors/ , https://www.bonfire.com/blog/best-t-shirt-colors-styles/ . Budgets: one per garment body, set at the units on hand when this file was derived, so the table starts by holding the current level rather than proposing a change nobody decided.",
